1
00:00:00,240 --> 00:00:02,320
Let's look at five ways that

2
00:00:02,320 --> 00:00:04,640
we can use the word 'set'.

3
00:00:04,640 --> 00:00:07,943
'Set' can mean 'ready', so:

4
00:00:07,943 --> 00:00:10,560
'Are you set? Then let's begin.'

5
00:00:10,560 --> 00:00:13,200
'A set' can mean 'a collection'.

6
00:00:13,200 --> 00:00:15,840
'This video is part of a set of

7
00:00:15,840 --> 00:00:17,520
English In A Minute videos.'

8
00:00:17,520 --> 00:00:21,040
'Set' can also mean 'put in place'.

9
00:00:21,040 --> 00:00:24,880
'I can set my glass on the table.'

10
00:00:24,880 --> 00:00:27,920
We can also 'set a date' and this

11
00:00:27,920 --> 00:00:30,591
means 'to arrange a date',

12
00:00:30,591 --> 00:00:31,680
for example:

13
00:00:31,680 --> 00:00:34,160
'We have set the date for our wedding.

14
00:00:34,160 --> 00:00:36,320
It's on the 1st of June'.

15
00:00:36,320 --> 00:00:38,720
If we are 'set on something',

16
00:00:38,720 --> 00:00:41,440
we are strongly in favour of it,

17
00:00:41,440 --> 00:00:44,720
for example: 'I am set on getting

18
00:00:44,720 --> 00:00:46,080
married in June.'

19
00:00:46,080 --> 00:00:48,800
And we can use 'set against' to

20
00:00:48,800 --> 00:00:49,680
say that we are

21
00:00:49,680 --> 00:00:53,520
'strongly against something', for example:

22
00:00:53,520 --> 00:00:57,440
'My parents are set against my wedding.'

23
00:00:57,440 --> 00:00:58,640
Oh no!

